    ('epfo-apfc-2016-q54', 54, 2016, 'January', 1, 'apfc', 'UPSC EPFO APFC', 'upsc-epfo', 'indian_polity_constitution_governance.parliament_state_legislatures.structure_composition', 'Indian Polity, Constitution & Governance', 'भारतीय राजव्यवस्था, संविधान एवं शासन', 'Parliament & State Legislatures', 'Parliament & State Legislatures', 'Structure & Composition (Lok Sabha, Rajya Sabha, Vidhan Sabha, Vidhan Parishad; Qualification & Disqualification - Article 102, Representation of the People Act 1951, Anti-Defection Law / 10th Schedule)', 'Structure & Composition (Lok Sabha, Rajya Sabha, Vidhan Sabha, Vidhan Parishad; Qualification & Disqualification - Article 102, Representation of the People Act 1951, Anti-Defection Law / 10th Schedule)', 'medium', ARRAY['Indian Polity', 'Constitution of India', 'Governance']::TEXT[], NULL, NULL, 'Parliament can make laws for any part of India to implement international treaties', 'अंतरराष्ट्रीय संधियाँ लागू करने के लिए संसद भारत के किसी भाग हेतु कानून बना सकती है', 'With consent of all States', 'With consent of a majority of States', 'With consent of concerned States', 'Without consent of any State', 'सभी राज्यों की सहमति से', 'बहुमत राज्यों की सहमति से', 'संबंधित राज्यों की सहमति से', 'किसी राज्य की सहमति के बिना', 'd', 'Correct Answer: (d) Without consent of any State

Explanation: Article 253 empowers Parliament to legislate for implementing international agreements, including matters otherwise falling within State legislative fields.

The Article does not require unanimous, majority or affected-State consent; options a–c add conditions not stipulated there.

