    ('epfo-apfc-2002-q22', 22, 2002, NULL, 1, 'apfc', 'UPSC EPFO APFC', 'upsc-epfo', 'language_proficiency_grammar_communication.reading_comprehension_sentence_correction_verbal_ability.verbal_ability_sentence_rearrangement', 'Language Proficiency, Grammar & Communication (general Kannada & General English)', 'भाषा प्रवीणता, व्याकरण एवं संचार', 'Reading Comprehension, Sentence Correction & Verbal Ability', 'Reading Comprehension, Sentence Correction & Verbal Ability', 'Verbal Ability & Sentence Rearrangement (Para Jumbles / Sentence Reordering - Identifying Opening Sentences, Connecting Links, Pronoun References, Concluding Sentences; Cloze Test - Contextual Word Fillers; Sentence Completion & Double Fillers)', 'Verbal Ability & Sentence Rearrangement (Para Jumbles / Sentence Reordering - Identifying Opening Sentences, Connecting Links, Pronoun References, Concluding Sentences; Cloze Test - Contextual Word Fillers; Sentence Completion & Double Fillers)', 'medium', ARRAY['General Studies', 'EPFO', 'General English', 'Language', 'Language Proficiency']::TEXT[], 'Directions for questions 19–23: Read the following passage and answer the questions based on it.

Passage:
When you buy a car, examine carefully the important features of the model you are considering. Many a buyer of the latest models has got into trouble by buying a car too broad or too long for his garage. Furthermore, a long car is much harder to manoeuvre in traffic and much more difficult to park. Another important feature is the comfort afforded. Are the seats nice and durably upholstered? Is there enough glass area to give the driver a good view in all directions, particularly to the rear? It should be remembered that the heavier and more powerful a car is, the more expensive it will be to operate. High powered motors require expensive high-octane petrol. The greater weight means greater tyre wear and enlarged brakes. The old cliché is still true; it is not the initial cost but the upkeep which matters!', 'Directions for questions 19–23: Read the following passage and answer the questions based on it.

Passage:
When you buy a car, examine carefully the important features of the model you are considering. Many a buyer of the latest models has got into trouble by buying a car too broad or too long for his garage. Furthermore, a long car is much harder to manoeuvre in traffic and much more difficult to park. Another important feature is the comfort afforded. Are the seats nice and durably upholstered? Is there enough glass area to give the driver a good view in all directions, particularly to the rear? It should be remembered that the heavier and more powerful a car is, the more expensive it will be to operate. High powered motors require expensive high-octane petrol. The greater weight means greater tyre wear and enlarged brakes. The old cliché is still true; it is not the initial cost but the upkeep which matters!', 'It is very expensive to maintain a large car because it', 'It is very expensive to maintain a large car because it', 'is heavy', 'requires more space for parking', 'needs changing its tyres more frequently', 'needs more durable upholstery', 'is heavy', 'requires more space for parking', 'needs changing its tyres more frequently', 'needs more durable upholstery', 'c', 'Correct Answer: (c) needs changing its tyres more frequently

Explanation: Greater weight causes greater tyre wear and more frequent replacement expense.

Heaviness is the underlying cause, but c identifies the stated maintenance cost. Parking space and upholstery are not the specified mechanism.', 'सही उत्तर: (c) needs changing its tyres more frequently

व्याख्या: अधिक वजन से टायर ज्यादा घिसते हैं और बदलने का खर्च बढ़ता है।

वजन मूल कारण है, पर c रखरखाव खर्च सीधे बताता है। पार्किंग और जिल्द बताए तंत्र नहीं हैं।', NULL),
