    ('epfo-apfc-2012-q88', 88, 2012, 'September', 1, 'apfc', 'UPSC EPFO APFC', 'upsc-epfo', 'indian_economy_development.monetary_policy_banking_architecture', 'Indian Economy & Development', 'भारतीय अर्थव्यवस्था एवं विकास', 'Monetary Policy & Banking Architecture', 'Monetary Policy & Banking Architecture', 'Monetary Policy & Banking Architecture', 'Monetary Policy & Banking Architecture', 'medium', ARRAY['Indian Polity', 'Constitution of India', 'Governance', 'EPFO Verified PYQ']::TEXT[], NULL, NULL, 'Foreign inflows need sterilisation to control inflation. What does sterilisation mean here?', 'मुद्रास्फीति रोकने को विदेशी अंतर्वाह का निष्फलीकरण क्या है?', 'Keep counterfeit currency out of circulation', 'Account for black money', 'Withdraw equivalent local currency to maintain a desirable exchange rate', 'Comply with import-export rules', 'नकली मुद्रा रोकना', 'काले धन का लेखा', 'वांछनीय विनिमय दर हेतु बराबर स्थानीय मुद्रा वापस लेना', 'आयात-निर्यात नियम पालन', 'c', 'Correct Answer: (c) Withdraw equivalent local currency to maintain a desirable exchange rate

Explanation: Foreign-exchange purchases inject domestic liquidity. Sterilisation offsets it by withdrawing liquidity, commonly through sale of domestic securities, limiting monetary expansion.

Counterfeiting, tax evasion and trade compliance are separate issues. Exchange-rate management and liquidity sterilisation are related but distinct policy operations.

